"Figure caption: Schematic illustration of how ERS-attenuating iLMPs enhance exogenous mRNA translation for in situ tissue regeneration. (A) Chemical structure and ERS alleviation efficacy of iVES. (B) General schematic illustration of iLNP fabrication through microfluidic mixing with water and the organic phase. (C, D) Microfluidic fabrication of in situ injectable iLMPs. (E) ERS attenuation and translation improvement mechanisms of BMP2@iLMPs.\n\nFigure caption: Synthesis of iVES and preparation of iLNPs. (A) Synthesis route of iVES from VES. (B) HRMS of iVES. (C) 1H NMR spectrum of iVES. (D) HPLC elution curves of iVES and VES, as well as iVES under acidic conditions (iVES+H+). (E) The release of VES from iVES. (F) The pKa value of iVES, defined as the pH at half-maximal fluorescence intensity. (G) Different formulations (A1-A4) of iLNPs. (H) Schematic illustration of BMP2@iLNP preparation using the five components. Zeta potential (I), size (J) and encapsulation efficiency (EE%) (K) of the optimized BMP2@iLNPs prepared through a microfluidic device at a high pump rate. (L) mRNA binding analysis of BMP2@iLNPs (A3) performed by agarose gel electrophoresis. (M) TEM image of BMP2@iLNPs (A3) stained with a uranyl acetate solution (scale bar, 200 nm). The experiments were carried out 3 times. The data are presented as the means ± SDs.\n\nFigure caption: Preparation and characterization of iLMPs. (A) Microfluidic fabrication of injectable HAMA microspheres. (B) Preparation of iLMPs from HAMA microspheres with iLNPs adsorption on the surface. (C) Optical microscopy images of the microspheres after freeze-drying and swelling. (D) The swelling ratio of iLMPs. (E, F) SEM images of iLMPs. (G) LSCM image of DiL-stained LNPs loaded with iLMPs and illustration of iLMPs. (H) Morphological changes in iLMPs observed during degradation tests. (I) Degradation profile of iLMPs. (J) Release curve of iLNPs from iLMPs. The experiments were carried out 3 times. The data are presented as the means ± SDs.\n\nFigure caption: In vitro biocompatibility evaluations of iLMPs. (A) Live/dead analysis of MC3T3-E1 cells after different treatments for 24 and 72 h via calcein-AM/PI staining. Scale bar, 50 μm. (B) Representative images of the different groups in the scratch experiment. Images of the migration of MC3T3-E1 cells after different treatments. Scale bar, 50 μm. (C) Semiquantitative analysis of the scratch test results. (D) CCK-8 assay to detect the cytotoxicity of B/iLMPs. *n.s.: not significant (p > 0.05).\n\nFigure caption: In vitro evaluation of iLMP-mediated ERS attenuation. (A) Mechanism of reduced mRNA translation efficiency during ERS. (B) Western blot analysis of Bip, p-PERK and p-eIF2α protein expression in MC3T3-E1 cells after different treatments. (C–E) Semiquantitative analysis of Bip, p-PERK and p-eIF2α expression by Western blot analysis. (F) Schematic diagram of the OPP assay for detecting ribosomal translation levels. (G) Fluorescence images of OPP assay results in MC3T3-E1 cells and immunofluorescence staining of ROS production in MC3T3-E1 cells after transfection with iLMPs. Scale bar, 50 μm. (H, I) Fluorescence images and semiquantitative analysis of mCherry-positive MC3T3-E1 cells transfected with iLMPs obtained by flow cytometry. Scale bar, 50 μm **p < 0.01.\n\nFigure caption: In vitro evaluation of iLMP-mediated osteogenesis. (A) Western blot analysis of BMP-2 and RUNX2 protein expression in MC3T3-E1 cells. (B, C) Semiquantitative analysis of BMP-2 and RUNX2 expression by Western blot analysis. (D–F) Osteogenic differentiation assessed by ALP staining and semiquantitative analysis. Scale bar, 50 μm. (G–I) Osteogenic differentiation assessed by Alizarin red S staining and semiquantitative analysis. Scale bar, 50 μm **p < 0.01, n.s.: not significant (p > 0.05). ALP: alkaline phosphatase. ARS: Alizarin red s.\n\nFigure caption: Establishment of the animal model and pathological analysis. (A) Schematic illustration of SANFH establishment by methylprednisolone sodium (MPA), administration modality, and therapeutic timeline. (B) Representative images of H&E-stained femoral head samples. Scale bars:1 mm (top), 100 μm (bottom). (C) Representative images of Masson's trichrome-stained samples from the different groups. Upper scale bar, 1 mm; lower scale bar, 100 μm. (D) Micro-CT images of the sagittal and transverse views of femoral head samples. (E) Ficat stages of femoral head necrosis after different treatments. (F–J) Quantitative micro-CT analysis of the bone mineralization density (BMD), trabecular number (Tb.N), bone volume fraction (BV/TV), trabecular separation (Tb.Sp), and trabecular thickness (Tb.Th) of femoral head samples. *p < 0.05, **p < 0.01, n.s.: not significant (p > 0.05).\n\nFigure caption: In vivo evaluation of B/iLMP-mediated ERS attenuation. (A) Mechanism of decreased mRNA translation efficiency during ERS. (B–D) Immunohistochemical staining for ERS-related proteins (Bip, p-PERK and p-eIF2α) in femoral head samples. Scale bars: 200 μm (top), 50 μm (bottom). (E–G) Semiquantitative analysis of Bip, p-PERK and p-eIF2α protein levels via immunohistochemical staining. *p < 0.05, **p < 0.01.\n\nFigure caption: In vivo evaluation of B/iLMP-mediated bone regeneration. (A) Schematic illustration of bone regeneration. (B, C) Immunohistochemical staining for BMP-2 and RUNX2 in femoral head samples. Scale bars: 40 μm (top) and 12 μm (bottom). (D, E) Semiquantitative analysis of BMP-2 and RUNX2 expression by immunohistochemical staining. *p < 0.05, **p < 0.01, n.s.: not significant (p > 0.05)."